      "text": "To solve for the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we need to find the largest integer \\(N\\) such that \\(N = 1000a + 100b + 10c + d\\) where \\(N \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\\), and \\(0 \\leq a, b, c, d \\leq 9\\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the remainder when \\(1000 \\times 6 + 100 \\times 5 + 10 \\times 4 + d\\) is divided by 22. We can simplify this as:\n\\[1000 \\times 6 + 100 \\times 5 + 10 \\times 4 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{22}\\]\nSince \\(6 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{22}\\), we need \\(10 \\times 4 + d \\equiv -6 \\pmod{22}\\). Simplifying further:\n\\[40 + d \\equiv -6 \\pmod{22}\\]\n\\[d \\equiv -6 + 40 \\pmod{22}\\]\n\\[d \\equiv 34 \\pmod{22}\\]\n\\[d \\equiv 2 \\pmod{22}\\]\nSo, \\(d = 2\\).\n\nNow, we need to find the largest possible value of \\(N\\) such that \\(N \\mod 22 = 15\\). The largest 4-digit number is 9999. We can express 9999 as \\(9999 = 490 \\times 22 + r\\), where \\(0 \\leq r < 22\\). Calculating:\n\\[9999 = 490 \\times 22 + 15\\]\nThis means the largest 4-digit number that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is 9999. To confirm, we check:\n\\[9999 \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\\]\nSince \\(9999 - 490 \\times 22 = 15\\), the largest 4-digit number congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is indeed 9999.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{9999}\\]",
